00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.950 align:start position:0%
 
hi i'm roy rasheen and i'm the director  of breeding insight and today i'm going  to be talking about building breeding  support for usda ars  horticultural crops  so breeders have a number of challenges  to get to where  they can release newer and better  varieties faster and unlike most of the  annual or major crops horticultural  crops face additional challenges  in terms of logistical challenges  they're running with a small budget a  small workforce generally it's temporary  a lot of temporary labor and by its very  nature the crops are more  labor-intensive in their management and  production  in terms of technical  challenges new tech is really hard for a  small program to adopt they don't know  where to start they're not sure if it's  the right technology to meet their needs  they have no idea if the maintenance  cost is something they can afford and  they're not really always sure how it's  going to fit into their workflow  environmentalism affects everybody and  the  droughts floods seasonal fluctuations  and climate change are affecting all  breeders globally however if you're not  breeding for an annual crop you have to  be thinking much further ahead and need  a bigger source of genetic diversity to  combat the things that are coming  because you're always breeding a couple  of years behind what's actually  happening right now  and lastly horticultural crops have a  lot of biological issues so they may  have a really complex genome  they have may have a longer life cycle a  biennial or perennial life cycle a lot  of self-incompatibility may still exist  and they may even have separate genders  and the most of the biggest problem is  really the time to maturity so  it takes you know five to six years for  a citrus plant to actually produce fruit  so you're always working behind the  clock  and when i think about breeding programs  i think about three different types and  the first type is phenotypic selection  where breeder knowledge is far more  important to the decision process than  genetics  in this case  breeding  is is done by eye and it takes a lot of  time to perfect that breeder's art  it relies on very high numbers where  you're putting out thousands of  individuals and calling down to maybe a  few thousand for advancement  and there's this is very effective it's  how most breeding has been uh  been done for you know generations and  years if you think about dogs or cats or  any other kind of species where  phenotypic breeding is happening and it  is really easy to adopt it's very easy  to get started you could do it in your  garden at home  but it takes years to really perfect  and so  in terms of some examples  citrus blueberry cranberry and again  most horticultural crops fall into this  category  the next step up is marker assisted  selection where breeders knowledge is  still being is still critical and and  central to decision making but it's  being supplemented with information from  genetics  and  this is typically done for resistance  genes or other kinds of key  digestibility disease  genes where you want to be able to  screen very early in your populations  the things that do not have the markers  that you care about for a release  variety  and it does require you having markers  that are distributed genome wine and it  does  require that you understand that the  relationship of those markers to your  genes of interest  and so when a breeder considers moving  from phenotypic selection to marker  assisted selection there are real  barriers to entry for those breeders who  want to adopt that technology and it's  the culmination of all the logistical  technical and biological challenges that  are preventing technology adoption or  program growth  and the last stage is genomic selection  where the breeder's knowledge is still  important but it's superseded by the  genetic information so the breeder  knowledge is typically used more towards  the end of the breeding program when it  comes time to  make a selection of between a couple of  different cultivars as to what would be  released  and genetics is really helping to  streamline the program much earlier in  the in the breeding cycle  and genomic selection requires both high  quality phenotypic data for the traits  of interest and genome-wide genotyping  of individuals in your population  again when a breeder decides they want  to move to more genomic selection to get  more efficiency in their program there  are new barriers to entry that pamper  that transition  and we get the question from a lot of  our phenotypic readers why do i need to  add genomics and it really comes down to  that sequencing technology is accessible  and affordable for all species  and breeders have a lot of information  they have to collect assimilate and act  upon in very short windows of time with  fixed constraints  and breeders don't get to see if they  were right until it's too late to change  course  so by supplementing the breeder's eye  with genomic data and analytics and  prediction models we can free breeders  from a lot of this grunt work  and release reduce the uncertainty while  still staying within the program's  constraints  really want to make sure that the  breeders get to do more breeding  so we need to understand what a breeder  needs to make good progress they need to  manage their breeding program have their  data all in one place  they need to have tools that help them  collect data in the field  they need to manage and store that trait  data and they need to manage samples  that they're collecting whether it's  leaf tissue for dna extraction or it's  post-harvest analysis  they want to have access to what's in  the usda germplasm banks is that's the  primary place they're going to go for  new native traits and pre-breeding  activities  they need to be able to store and  retrieve genetic data  and bring that data along with the trade  data together to produce these genomic  analyses to help make improved decisions  and to also do better reporting  so at breeding insight we are here  piloting with six usda specialty  breeding programs and we start by  getting to know the programs and  listening to them about what their wants  and needs are  we provide resources by making  connections to  getting them genotypic platforms to  providing vendors to help them uh keep  the costs down we help them manage that  data  we also help them manage and accumulate  phenotypic data of higher quality and we  provide  reading support and consultation so  we're here as their partner in their  technology adoption journey  and we also help deliver software and  we're working in a public sphere so we  are creating open source software and  we're not looking to reinvent the wheel  so we look at what's available  and then we decide what functionality is  not already available that needs to be  there for our breeder specific use cases  and then we create software to meet  those those gaps  and in order to make uh an in a holistic  system  we need to have back to seamless back to  back end communication so that data is  moving from one software component to  another without the need for manual  transfer  and we need to have an overlay of a ui  that looks really breeder friendly and  meets the needs of each individual  breeding program  so one thing we found by talking to our  breeders is that no matter what you're  breeding for whether it be  fish or  um  alfalfa that your fo breeders are  following a 12-month breeding cycle and  that means that things that are  happening in one part of the cycle are  not happening in another  so to help us understand that we broke  it down into the workflows that are  actually happening in time so breeders  are having to manage stocks and  pedigrees perform crosses and design and  manage trials at the beginning of the  cycle  they're having to capture phenotypes  whether it's on paper or audio or some  mobile device  they're having to evaluate and genotypes  and evaluate their phenotypes  all so that they can finally make  decisions and selections whether it's  genomic selection or marker assisted  selection and then finally their annual  reports so breeding insight is really  dedicated to creating a digital  ecosystem where breeders can manage  their workflows in time with the cycle  and flux of their own program in the  calendar year  so our initial platform  is starts with breedbase which has  general functionality and we believe is  going to be fairly species agnostic  we're partnering with fieldbook from the  phenoapps suite to do mobile phenotyping  on  uh android devices in the field  we're using gigwa for our backend  genomic  data  database storage  and we're going we're in underway  to connect with green global to allow  breeders to be able to seamlessly look  at what's in the green global databases  and to request uh materials and the way  we're connecting these disparate systems  is by using the breeding api or the  or what we call brappy and that's a  communication standard that allows  different software to communicate  information back and forth between each  other without the need for a human to  manually transfer data on excel files  so right now breeding insight is in the  pilot phase and what that means is that  it's an ars  initiative aimed at  ars breeders of specialty crop animals  and natural resources and we fall under  the national program direction of tim  reinhart  and right now we're only breeding for  three out of about 50 specialty crops  about two out of the 15 specialty  animals and only one of the natural  resource or of the 30 that are available  but we're hoping as we grow and expand  this program out that our vision for one  breeding insight really is to support  all the  specialty crops plus the national  germplasm repositories for pre-breeding  activities  all the specialty animals and any  natural resources that are available now  or will become breeding crops as as we  change the the scope of what we're  breeding for  and in order to do that we will still be  relying on the breeding insight core  that's at cornell  but ars is will be making a commitment  to distribute breeding insight  coordinators area-wide nationally so  that they can be  more more hands-on with the breeders as  they adopt technologies  and one of the ways ars is doing this is  by creating an ars readiness program  called bi on-ramp and it's for  non-specialty crop and animal breeders  that wouldn't qualify for breeding  insight because they're not specialty  crops  and it's also for ars specialty crop and  animal breeders that want or need a more  extended transition into bi and this one  to two year commitment really allows  them to get their trade ontology set up  to migrate data from paper to digital to  really start organizing their program  and making some decisions about how they  want to expand and build capacity in  their program  and really the story is is that together  bi and bi on ram can build better tools  for all public breeders because a lot of  the same things are needed both in ars  breeding programs as well as in public  breeding programs and because our  species are going to be diversified  we're going to be able to build the most  species agnostic system when we work  together  and with that i would like to thank my  whole entire team they do the work i'm  just the one getting to do the talking  our ars collaborators are fantastic  along with our oversight from our  national program leaders at the bottom  and our scientific advisory board we do  have a github all of our software is  available freely you're welcome to  go there and download  anything that's of interest to you thank  you

